=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the netfilter (iptables) FORWARD chain on router6. Four rules were
injected into that chain, all matching on inbound interface eth2 — the WAN-facing link (r6_r3)
that connects router6 to router3 and, through the wider RIP topology, to all of Location A
(172.16.10.0/24 / 172.16.20.0/24 where PC4 and PC5 live). Rule 1 permits only one very narrow
flow (TCP from 172.16.10.0/24 destined to 172.18.51.1 port 80); rules 2–4 then drop every
other TCP, UDP, and ICMP packet that enters on eth2 before it can be forwarded to eth0/eth1
toward mswitch2, switch9/10/11, and the 172.18.50.0/24 / 172.18.51.0/24 subnets (PC15–18,
laptop4/5). Because every packet originating from Location A must traverse the
R0→R1→R2→R3→R6.eth2 chain, the DROP rules silently discard those packets the moment they
arrive at router6, preventing any reply from ever reaching PC4 or PC5. Destinations reachable
via the alternative path through router5/router4 (e.g. PC11 at 192.168.200.11) are entirely
unaffected — confirming the fault is narrowly scoped to router6's eth2 ACL. Direct evidence:
the DROP rule 4 counter climbed from 4 packets (336 B) to 7 packets (588 B) in exact lockstep
with the three additional ICMP echo requests sent from router3 during testing, and router6 can
reach PC15 directly (local segment healthy). The root cause is that erroneous iptables DROP
rules were added to router6's FORWARD chain targeting inbound eth2, reversing the intended
access-control direction (the baseline policy was to restrict Location B traffic reaching
Location A, not the other way around). Fix: on router6, remove rules 2–4 from the FORWARD
chain (`iptables -D FORWARD 4; iptables -D FORWARD 3; iptables -D FORWARD 2`) to restore full
WAN→mswitch2 forwarding.
=== END ===
